WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.709 --> 00:00:02.377
I lived in New York for years,

00:00:02.377 --> 00:00:05.338
and I think it has some of
the best food in the world.

00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:07.924
I've invited my friend
Harry Kersh over from the UK

00:00:07.924 --> 00:00:09.342
to teach him how we do things

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:11.219
slightly differently over here.

00:00:11.219 --> 00:00:13.972
We'll be visiting four of
the top-rated bagel spots

00:00:13.972 --> 00:00:16.474
across the city all in one day

00:00:16.474 --> 00:00:19.686
to see if we can find
New York's best bagel.

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:21.479
This is "Food Tours."

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:26.609
Our first stop of the day is
at Utopia Bagels in Queens.

00:00:26.609 --> 00:00:30.238
We're at Utopia Bagels in
Queens on Utopia Parkway.

00:00:30.238 --> 00:00:32.615
Second-best Fountains of Wayne album.

00:00:32.615 --> 00:00:34.784
And the reason I took you
here is because this place,

00:00:34.784 --> 00:00:36.578
it's got dynamite bagels,

00:00:36.578 --> 00:00:37.787
and although we're off the beaten path,

00:00:37.787 --> 00:00:39.497
so maybe not a lot of
tourists come out here,

00:00:39.497 --> 00:00:40.540
don't get me wrong, buddy.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.459
They make so many bagels here.

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:44.836
Over 100,000 a week.

00:00:44.836 --> 00:00:45.628
That's a lot of bagels.

00:00:45.628 --> 00:00:49.215
Dude, they go through
2 tons of flour a day.

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:50.341
So many bagels!

00:00:50.341 --> 00:00:53.261
Joe: Dude, the oven they
got in there is from 1947,

00:00:53.261 --> 00:00:56.264
which of course was ...
during World War II?

00:00:56.264 --> 00:00:57.223
Just after.

00:00:57.223 --> 00:00:58.933
Joe: Yes.

00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:00.685
So I figured for the
day, every place we go,

00:01:00.685 --> 00:01:02.854
we're getting the
traditional New York City

00:01:02.854 --> 00:01:04.355
bagels and lox.
Harry: Perfect.

00:01:04.355 --> 00:01:05.857
Joe: Have you ever had
bagels and lox before?

00:01:05.857 --> 00:01:06.691
I have.

00:01:06.691 --> 00:01:09.194
In the UK we call it a salmon
and cream cheese bagel.

00:01:09.194 --> 00:01:10.361
Oh, my God.

00:01:11.446 --> 00:01:12.572
You have a lot to learn, my friend.

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:14.115
What do you think makes a good bagel?

00:01:14.115 --> 00:01:17.243
Harry: So, I'm looking for
a nice golden brown bagel.

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:19.037
I want it seasoned on both sides.

00:01:19.037 --> 00:01:20.121
So if we got, like, everything bagel,

00:01:20.121 --> 00:01:22.123
we need toppings on
the top and the bottom.

00:01:22.123 --> 00:01:23.374
Don't be skimping on it.

00:01:23.374 --> 00:01:25.085
Crackly crust on the outside,

00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:27.045
nice and chewy inside.

00:01:27.045 --> 00:01:28.797
And then the toppings have
got to be good as well.

00:01:28.797 --> 00:01:29.798
We need the salmon to be good,

00:01:29.798 --> 00:01:31.257
the cream cheese to be good.

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:32.425
Everything's got to work together.

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:33.343
Joe: Yes. I agree.

00:01:33.343 --> 00:01:36.012
For me, what I look for
is what tastes the best.

00:01:36.012 --> 00:01:38.014
That helps on a food show.

00:01:38.014 --> 00:01:39.724
Some places attempt to just overfill

00:01:39.724 --> 00:01:41.810
for the sake of overfilling it,

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:43.353
whereas actually, like, I don't want that.

00:01:43.353 --> 00:01:46.106
I'd rather have fewer toppings,

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:48.358
and therefore it holds together
much better when you eat it.

00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:49.734
I want everything in one bite.

00:01:49.734 --> 00:01:51.486
So don't be stacking.

00:01:51.486 --> 00:01:53.321
Interesting thing about bagels and lox,

00:01:53.321 --> 00:01:56.324
which I've heard before and
I cannot confirm or deny

00:01:56.324 --> 00:01:57.700
but it sounds like it's true,

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.327
and that's all we need for the internet,

00:01:59.327 --> 00:02:02.539
is that bagels and lox
became popular because

00:02:02.539 --> 00:02:05.250
it was a reaction to eggs
Benedict, which wasn't kosher.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:06.209
And you're half Jewish?

00:02:06.209 --> 00:02:07.210
I am half Jewish, yeah.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:08.795
Joe: Have you ever gone
kosher or done anything?

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:10.588
No, I'm not a very good Jew.

00:02:10.588 --> 00:02:12.674
I'm 50% Ashkenazi Jewish,

00:02:12.674 --> 00:02:15.927
which is the type of Jewish
where we kind of moved to

00:02:15.927 --> 00:02:19.305
Eastern Europe and then many
people then came to the US.

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:22.016
So, honestly, they've kind of
brought the bagels with them.

00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:23.601
So I feel like I'm experiencing, like,

00:02:23.601 --> 00:02:25.770
a spiritual homecoming with this episode.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:26.646
I'm very excited.

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:27.605
Joe: I'm starving.

00:02:27.605 --> 00:02:29.107
Harry: I'm so hungry.
Joe: All right.

00:02:29.107 --> 00:02:30.775
Hold the table. I'm
going to get some bagels.

00:02:30.775 --> 00:02:31.693
Harry: I got it.

00:02:38.783 --> 00:02:39.993
Joe: Pew!

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:41.911
Harry: Ooh!

00:02:41.911 --> 00:02:43.121
Joe: Look at this beast.
Harry: Ooh, mama!

00:02:43.121 --> 00:02:44.080
There you go.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.124
Harry: Man. Look at this thing!

00:02:46.124 --> 00:02:49.627
Look how much lox they gave us, bro.

00:02:49.627 --> 00:02:50.628
Harry: That is thick.

00:02:50.628 --> 00:02:51.671
Joe: This look good, right?

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:54.007
Incredible amount of lox, as you can see.

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:56.176
Tomato, capers, cream cheese.

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:58.011
Also got raw red onion on there.

00:02:58.011 --> 00:03:00.138
All on an everything bagel.

00:03:00.138 --> 00:03:01.556
First impressions, what do you think?

00:03:01.556 --> 00:03:04.934
Harry: This looks fantastic.
Very pretty to look at.

00:03:04.934 --> 00:03:07.353
Like you say, generous with the salmon.

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:10.607
I will say the tomato kind
of blends in with the salmon.

00:03:10.607 --> 00:03:12.025
So for a second I thought
it was just, like,

00:03:12.025 --> 00:03:13.985
a pure wedge of salmon.
Joe: Let's separate it.

00:03:13.985 --> 00:03:15.445
Harry: Some of it is tomato.
Joe: That's pretty good.

00:03:15.445 --> 00:03:16.863
Harry: Still pretty generous.

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:18.239
I'm not complaining.

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:20.950
Bagel's still a little bit
warm, which excites me a lot.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.327
You feeling that?
Joe: Fresh. I mean, these are

00:03:22.327 --> 00:03:24.787
straight out of the oven, the 1947 oven.

00:03:24.787 --> 00:03:25.622
Harry: Should we give it a try?

00:03:25.622 --> 00:03:26.748
Yeah. Oh, yeah.

00:03:29.667 --> 00:03:31.961
Mm. Mm!

00:03:31.961 --> 00:03:34.005
Oh, man.

00:03:34.005 --> 00:03:35.882
Joe: The crunch.
Harry: Mm-hmm.

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:37.800
That taste like a bagel you're
getting over there in London?

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.969
This is the first bagel
I've had in New York,

00:03:39.969 --> 00:03:41.763
and it already might be
better than any bagel

00:03:41.763 --> 00:03:42.722
I've had in London.

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.641
Believe it. You guys normally
get it with the capers

00:03:44.641 --> 00:03:46.726
and raw red onion and that stuff?

00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:49.896
Not really. Some delis
might do it to order.

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:51.231
I'd say the standard one in the UK

00:03:51.231 --> 00:03:53.107
if you ask for, like, a bagel and lox

00:03:53.107 --> 00:03:55.026
or a bagel with salmon and cream cheese

00:03:55.026 --> 00:03:57.111
will just be salmon and cream cheese,

00:03:57.111 --> 00:03:59.906
without the onion, without the
tomato, without the capers.

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:01.741
It's very much just a plain sandwich.

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:03.034
But I'm liking all these toppings.

00:04:03.034 --> 00:04:04.160
I do think they work together.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:05.119
Joe: Yeah, it's great.

00:04:05.119 --> 00:04:07.872
I would've never thought
before having this —

00:04:07.872 --> 00:04:10.500
the, like, fish for breakfast
would kind of turn me off.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:13.294
But that's amazing.
Right? It's so delicious.

00:04:13.294 --> 00:04:14.462
Harry: I'd say fish for
breakfast isn't something

00:04:14.462 --> 00:04:16.923
that's super uncommon to Europeans.

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:20.093
I think in Jewish culture,
in Eastern European culture,

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:22.387
and even in Scottish culture,
like, kippers for breakfast,

00:04:22.387 --> 00:04:24.222
you will find fish on the
menu at a breakfast place.

00:04:24.222 --> 00:04:25.682
So that's not super weird to us.

00:04:25.682 --> 00:04:27.308
But yeah, this really, really works.

00:04:27.308 --> 00:04:28.142
Joe: Yeah.

00:04:28.142 --> 00:04:30.770
Harry: I was worried when I
saw that tomato is in this.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:31.604
Generally speaking,

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:33.564
not a huge fan of tomatoes in sandwiches,

00:04:33.564 --> 00:04:35.275
but this really works.

00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:37.777
It's actually got some crunch to it.

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:39.028
A little bit of flavor.

00:04:39.028 --> 00:04:41.072
I think they've actually
seasoned it, which is crucial.

00:04:41.072 --> 00:04:43.116
Joe: Yeah. As you can tell, it's clutch

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:45.785
for places like this, the fact
that they make the bagels,

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:47.245
they're just making them all day,

00:04:47.245 --> 00:04:49.163
so they're getting them
right out of the oven.

00:04:49.163 --> 00:04:52.667
At other places, they got them
morning or the night before.

00:04:52.667 --> 00:04:55.461
These bagels, this is amazing
how great these bagels are.

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:57.255
You can really, really
taste the freshness.

00:04:57.255 --> 00:04:59.882
You bite into it and there's
steam coming out of it.

00:04:59.882 --> 00:05:02.385
Joe: They're making 15,000 of these a day.

00:05:02.385 --> 00:05:04.595
Harry: That's so many.
Joe: Right? That's a lot.

00:05:04.595 --> 00:05:05.930
That's really good to
know that you can come in

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:08.558
pretty much any time of day
and get a fresh bagel, though.

00:05:08.558 --> 00:05:09.392
It does make a difference.

00:05:09.392 --> 00:05:10.226
Joe: That's how they do it here. Yeah.

00:05:10.226 --> 00:05:13.479
And anyone will tell
you, the breakfast meal,

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:14.647
you gotta pair with a beverage.

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:18.276
I of course went with coffee,
and you of course got, yeah.

00:05:18.276 --> 00:05:20.570
How does that pair with the blue Gatorade?

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:23.281
Perfectly. So well.

00:05:23.281 --> 00:05:24.115
When in Rome, you know?

00:05:24.115 --> 00:05:25.408
Is this a European thing right here?

00:05:25.408 --> 00:05:26.784
Gatorade for breakfast?

00:05:26.784 --> 00:05:28.077
This is not, no.

00:05:28.077 --> 00:05:29.704
This is just me being a little bit sleepy.

00:05:29.704 --> 00:05:31.039
We're like pro athletes, man.

00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:32.498
We've got to eat our way
through New York today.

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:34.292
We need as many
electrolytes as we can get.

00:05:34.292 --> 00:05:35.710
Yeah. Yeah. I agree.

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:38.921
I will say, I think this
is a very, very good bagel.

00:05:38.921 --> 00:05:40.298
It had all the elements that I wanted.

00:05:40.298 --> 00:05:42.091
The bagel itself was fantastic.

00:05:42.091 --> 00:05:44.886
Crunchy on the outside,
chewy on the inside.

00:05:44.886 --> 00:05:47.096
Everything held together
pretty well, despite the fact

00:05:47.096 --> 00:05:49.015
that they were generous
but not too generous.

00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:49.849
They didn't stack.

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:52.310
Harry: They didn't stack. No stacking.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:55.146
So yeah, I think this might
be one to beat, you know?

00:05:55.146 --> 00:05:56.064
It's a good start.

00:05:56.064 --> 00:05:57.857
Well, I'm ready to hit it. You
want to go to the next place?

00:05:57.857 --> 00:05:59.359
Harry: I think so.
Joe: Let's go.

00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:02.362
Our next location has been
in the New York bagel game

00:06:02.362 --> 00:06:04.655
for over 32 years.

00:06:04.655 --> 00:06:05.698
Liberty Bagels.

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:09.077
All right. Look at me.

00:06:09.077 --> 00:06:11.162
Liberty Bagel, Queens.

00:06:11.162 --> 00:06:14.374
Not to be confused with the
location that's in Manhattan.

00:06:14.374 --> 00:06:15.333
This is the other one.

00:06:15.333 --> 00:06:16.667
Or the Statue of Liberty.

00:06:17.794 --> 00:06:18.628
Different place.

00:06:18.628 --> 00:06:20.588
And we're currently not
inside the Statue of Liberty.

00:06:20.588 --> 00:06:21.923
I want make it clear, that. I know you had

00:06:21.923 --> 00:06:23.800
asked if I had gone there.
Harry: Just to clarify. Yep.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:25.259
Joe: We're nowhere near it, I don't think.

00:06:25.259 --> 00:06:28.429
This spot, fantastic
bagels. I love this place.

00:06:28.429 --> 00:06:31.057
Also, I noticed on the menu
they have a rainbow bagel.

00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:33.810
You probably saw that all
over the grams of Insta.

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:35.019
Yeah, I mean, I'm seeing posters.

00:06:35.019 --> 00:06:36.646
It says the "home of the rainbow bagel."

00:06:36.646 --> 00:06:38.815
Were these guys, like,
one of the first to do it?

00:06:38.815 --> 00:06:39.899
No clue.

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:41.067
[Harry laughs]

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:42.485
Kind of like how you see
the signs that's like,

00:06:42.485 --> 00:06:44.737
"Best in New York City,"
and the bagel shops,

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:46.697
a lot of them are like,
"Home of the rainbow bagel."

00:06:46.697 --> 00:06:48.241
We're definitely getting
another bagel with the lox,

00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:49.409
the cream cheese, the
capers, the whole thing.

00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:52.078
I also do want to get a
rainbow bagel for you to try.

00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:55.331
I mean, it's like, it became
a New York City institution,

00:06:55.331 --> 00:06:56.165
for better or for worse.

00:06:56.165 --> 00:06:57.083
So we'll get one of those as well.

00:06:57.083 --> 00:07:00.670
Yeah, the rainbow bagel's
kind of like a 2017 gimmick.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:01.712
But, you know,

00:07:01.712 --> 00:07:04.090
feel like Insider specifically
owes a lot to that.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:05.716
Yes, they do, and I was part of that,

00:07:05.716 --> 00:07:07.760
and you're welcome, and
I'm sorry? Whatever.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.178
We can tell the viewers
at home we actually have

00:07:09.178 --> 00:07:12.473
a conference room in our
office called Rainbow Bagel.

00:07:12.473 --> 00:07:14.642
That is how deeply the Insider connection

00:07:14.642 --> 00:07:15.560
with rainbow bagels run.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:17.437
So yeah, I guess we can
try one of those as well.

00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:19.147
But the lox is the main event.

00:07:19.147 --> 00:07:20.022
That'll be fun too.

00:07:20.022 --> 00:07:21.482
Joe: So, I'm going on what's
on the picture, right?

00:07:21.482 --> 00:07:23.484
So this one I want to
point out has scallions

00:07:23.484 --> 00:07:25.778
and scallion cream cheese,
a favorite of mine,

00:07:25.778 --> 00:07:28.448
but it does not have
red onion. OK with that?

00:07:28.448 --> 00:07:31.451
Also comes on the poppy seed bun,

00:07:31.451 --> 00:07:34.454
so all urine tests are off.

00:07:34.454 --> 00:07:37.874
Good. I will not let anyone
test my pee in the next few days

00:07:37.874 --> 00:07:40.001
just to be on the safe side. Uh-huh.

00:07:40.001 --> 00:07:40.835
I'm excited.

00:07:40.835 --> 00:07:42.753
Joe: I picked this location because

00:07:42.753 --> 00:07:46.632
at their other location,
in Manhattan, is considered

00:07:46.632 --> 00:07:51.387
the best bagel in New
York City by Tripadvisor.

00:07:51.387 --> 00:07:53.973
And I couldn't imagine it
tasting that much different

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:55.057
there than here.

00:07:55.057 --> 00:07:56.225
But the good people at Tripadvisor

00:07:56.225 --> 00:07:59.645
said that Liberty Bagels are No. 1.

00:07:59.645 --> 00:08:01.772
We're going to see if Joe
Public knows their stuff

00:08:01.772 --> 00:08:03.065
when it comes to bagels, then.

00:08:03.065 --> 00:08:04.692
If there's one thing I've learned

00:08:04.692 --> 00:08:07.028
in my years on this planet

00:08:07.028 --> 00:08:09.322
is that what everyone says on the internet

00:08:09.322 --> 00:08:11.157
is probably correct all the time.

00:08:11.157 --> 00:08:13.075
I think that is absolutely true.

00:08:13.075 --> 00:08:16.120
I like it in here. Nice
vibe, friendly staff.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:17.622
I'm excited to try the bagels.

00:08:20.208 --> 00:08:21.459
Joe: Feast your eyes on this, my guy.

00:08:21.459 --> 00:08:23.628
Oh, ho-ho-ho-ho-hm-mm!

00:08:23.628 --> 00:08:27.256
They definitely made it
more obvious that the tomato

00:08:27.256 --> 00:08:30.134
and the salmon are two different zones.

00:08:30.134 --> 00:08:32.386
Yeah. Distinct layers there.

00:08:32.386 --> 00:08:34.680
Joe: Again, a lot of people,
they think it's quality

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.098
if it's piled high in the fish.

00:08:36.098 --> 00:08:37.433
You want everything in one bite.

00:08:37.433 --> 00:08:38.768
I don't want just one bite of fish.

00:08:38.768 --> 00:08:39.894
I don't want to OD on the fish.

00:08:39.894 --> 00:08:41.020
I want a taste of everything.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:42.522
Quality over quantity, I think.

00:08:42.522 --> 00:08:43.356
Yeah, it's important.

00:08:43.356 --> 00:08:44.732
Joe: Yeah. I think proportion-wise —

00:08:44.732 --> 00:08:46.359
and very generous with the cream cheese,

00:08:46.359 --> 00:08:49.445
also what I like. Capers and scallions.

00:08:49.445 --> 00:08:53.115
Harry: And this thing is
covered in poppy seeds.

00:08:53.115 --> 00:08:55.785
Top and bottom, good coverage.

00:08:55.785 --> 00:08:57.453
Joe: See that there? Ready to jump in?

00:08:57.453 --> 00:08:58.371
Harry: I'm so ready.

00:08:58.371 --> 00:08:59.330
Joe: Let's do it.
Harry: Let's go, man.

00:09:00.665 --> 00:09:01.666
Uh-oh!

00:09:05.419 --> 00:09:07.088
Had a bit of a — oh.

00:09:09.006 --> 00:09:10.466
Mine did smoosh a little bit.

00:09:12.385 --> 00:09:13.886
Harry: Little bit of smooshing.

00:09:13.886 --> 00:09:16.305
I'm also getting poppy seeds everywhere.

00:09:17.473 --> 00:09:19.475
Tomato's good. Quite firm.

00:09:19.475 --> 00:09:20.685
Good bite to the tomato.

00:09:20.685 --> 00:09:22.061
The bagel's fantastic.

00:09:22.061 --> 00:09:22.895
Harry: The bagel is really good.

00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:23.854
It's a fresh bagel.

00:09:23.854 --> 00:09:28.401
Yep. I'd say maybe not the
crunchiest on the outside,

00:09:28.401 --> 00:09:30.069
but the inner texture
is really, really good.

00:09:30.069 --> 00:09:32.572
That's got that chew
that you know and love.

00:09:32.572 --> 00:09:34.323
Joe: Now that we've had a
bite, I want to say that

00:09:34.323 --> 00:09:37.785
I'm a bit biased towards
scallion cream cheese

00:09:37.785 --> 00:09:39.996
and the scallion versus the red onion.

00:09:39.996 --> 00:09:41.872
I'm not saying that it's
my favorite everywhere,

00:09:41.872 --> 00:09:45.209
but knowing that made this more
of an enjoyable bite for me.

00:09:45.209 --> 00:09:47.295
I do love red onion, don't get me wrong.

00:09:47.295 --> 00:09:48.462
Scallions, I like.

00:09:48.462 --> 00:09:50.590
Yeah, I don't think I miss the red onion

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:51.757
at all, to be honest.

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:55.761
Joe: Because you're getting
that onioniness, that savoriness

00:09:55.761 --> 00:09:59.265
from two locations. Two ingredients.

00:09:59.265 --> 00:10:02.602
Little one-two allium punch there.

00:10:02.602 --> 00:10:06.272
There's no lack of want when
it comes to oniony flavor,

00:10:06.272 --> 00:10:07.106
is that what I'm trying to say?

00:10:07.106 --> 00:10:09.692
What I'm tasting the
least though is the fish.

00:10:09.692 --> 00:10:11.736
I'm not getting a lot
of the salmon in here.

00:10:11.736 --> 00:10:12.570
Let me get another bite.

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:13.529
Harry: I kind of disagree.

00:10:13.529 --> 00:10:15.448
I feel like it's got a
reasonably strong flavor.

00:10:15.448 --> 00:10:17.575
I don't know. So, like, in the UK,

00:10:17.575 --> 00:10:19.327
if you ever get a salmon
and cream cheese bagel,

00:10:19.327 --> 00:10:21.412
you're going to get smoked salmon.

00:10:21.412 --> 00:10:23.080
Is that an everywhere thing in the US

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:24.206
or is that kind of optional?

00:10:24.206 --> 00:10:26.292
It's not. It's to the place's discretion.

00:10:26.292 --> 00:10:27.126
I'm glad you asked that.

00:10:27.126 --> 00:10:27.960
So it's not always going to be smoked.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:29.128
I don't think it's smoked here, is it?

00:10:29.128 --> 00:10:30.588
Harry: Maybe quite mildly.

00:10:30.588 --> 00:10:33.174
Definitely not a strong smoky flavor,

00:10:33.174 --> 00:10:35.635
but I do think it's got
a good flavor to it.

00:10:35.635 --> 00:10:37.136
Maybe it's getting slightly lost

00:10:37.136 --> 00:10:39.180
in the scallion cream cheese, for example.

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:40.014
Joe: Yeah, that could be it.

00:10:40.014 --> 00:10:42.016
And like I say, they were
quite generous on the capers,

00:10:42.016 --> 00:10:43.392
which does mean that when
you bite into one of those

00:10:43.392 --> 00:10:45.186
for a second, it's like, boom.
That's all you can taste.

00:10:45.186 --> 00:10:47.229
I feel the capers stand
out more in this one.

00:10:47.229 --> 00:10:49.774
I'm not quite sure why
or how I feel about that.

00:10:49.774 --> 00:10:52.151
I think in general the
ratio is pretty good though.

00:10:52.151 --> 00:10:53.235
I'm not that mad about the ratio.

00:10:53.235 --> 00:10:55.154
Look at the cream cheese, man. Generous.

00:10:55.154 --> 00:10:56.322
Joe: They were generous
with the cream cheese,

00:10:56.322 --> 00:10:57.948
which is always nice.

00:10:57.948 --> 00:10:59.241
But that's making it squishy, man.

00:10:59.241 --> 00:11:00.242
You're squishing.

00:11:00.242 --> 00:11:01.994
I quite like when you get a cream cheese

00:11:01.994 --> 00:11:04.372
and it's quite sour and a bit tangy.

00:11:04.372 --> 00:11:06.874
I'd say this one's more
on the mild, creamy side.

00:11:06.874 --> 00:11:08.918
That's really funny because
I'm the exact opposite.

00:11:08.918 --> 00:11:11.295
I want the cream cheese
to be, like, just fine,

00:11:11.295 --> 00:11:13.714
because to me I want the bagel
to be the star of the show.

00:11:13.714 --> 00:11:15.383
So is this more up your
street and you're quite happy

00:11:15.383 --> 00:11:18.010
with the way that this cream cheese works?

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:20.888
I am, as far as the way
that the cream cheese

00:11:20.888 --> 00:11:22.098
does or does not stand out.

00:11:22.098 --> 00:11:24.141
I think, see, I'm just,
I'm, like, a savory onion,

00:11:24.141 --> 00:11:26.560
I'm an oniony boy. All right?

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:28.688
My persona is just onion.

00:11:28.688 --> 00:11:30.022
So you're an oniony boy.

00:11:30.022 --> 00:11:32.400
I'm more of a tang boy, I would say.

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:33.734
I'm more of a tangy boy.

00:11:33.734 --> 00:11:35.611
I want a bit of tang
from the cream cheese.

00:11:35.611 --> 00:11:36.946
The capers, I like.

00:11:36.946 --> 00:11:38.739
Extra tang, you know.

00:11:38.739 --> 00:11:40.282
You ate — come on, man.

00:11:40.282 --> 00:11:42.368
Harry: I couldn't stop myself,
man. I couldn't stop myself.

00:11:42.368 --> 00:11:43.285
It was a good bagel.

00:11:43.285 --> 00:11:44.995
RIP the last place we have to go to,

00:11:44.995 --> 00:11:45.830
because you're just going to be like,

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:48.290
"How is it?" "Fine. Let's go home."

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:50.334
All right. You couldn't come to New York

00:11:50.334 --> 00:11:53.087
without trying a rainbow bagel.

00:11:53.087 --> 00:11:54.130
Hang on.

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:56.424
I didn't say the rainbow bagel's good.

00:11:56.424 --> 00:11:58.092
It just became uniquely New York

00:11:58.092 --> 00:12:00.386
for maybe the wrong reasons.

00:12:00.386 --> 00:12:02.054
It was a viral sensation.

00:12:02.054 --> 00:12:04.265
So, wa-pow.
Harry: Oh, here we go.

00:12:04.265 --> 00:12:07.643
Joe: Well, and also, I
got sweet birthday cake.

00:12:07.643 --> 00:12:08.894
Harry: Oh that's, like, vibrant.

00:12:08.894 --> 00:12:11.731
Joe: I know, right? Ugh.
Harry: Oh, man.

00:12:11.731 --> 00:12:12.773
Look at this thing!

00:12:13.566 --> 00:12:15.651
Charlie: All of those colors 
are banned in the UK.

00:12:15.651 --> 00:12:18.154
Joe: Yeah. You're going to
eat this and then be, like,

00:12:18.154 --> 00:12:21.615
really aggressive for some unknown reason.

00:12:21.615 --> 00:12:22.867
More hyperactive than I've ever been.

00:12:22.867 --> 00:12:23.993
Joe: You can't get this
red in your country.

00:12:23.993 --> 00:12:25.327
So you say you've had this before?

00:12:25.327 --> 00:12:26.162
Joe: Yeah, I have.

00:12:26.162 --> 00:12:28.539
Harry: And this is the
birthday cake cream cheese?

00:12:28.539 --> 00:12:30.416
Joe: Yeah, little more food
coloring for you, my guy.

00:12:30.416 --> 00:12:32.126
Harry: I can't say I'm
thrilled about this,

00:12:32.126 --> 00:12:33.335
but let's give it a try.

00:12:35.713 --> 00:12:36.922
Joe: Ew.

00:12:36.922 --> 00:12:38.966
Like frosting.

00:12:38.966 --> 00:12:39.884
Harry: Mm-hmm.

00:12:39.884 --> 00:12:41.677
Joe: Who doesn't like
a bagel with frosting?

00:12:41.677 --> 00:12:42.762
Harry: I don't actually hate it

00:12:42.762 --> 00:12:44.805
as much as I thought I would.

00:12:44.805 --> 00:12:46.932
It's like a dessert bagel, right?

00:12:46.932 --> 00:12:49.727
Joe: We're adding frosting.
It's like a slice of cake.

00:12:49.727 --> 00:12:51.353
I certainly couldn't
handle one in the morning.

00:12:51.353 --> 00:12:52.688
I certainly would not be, like,

00:12:52.688 --> 00:12:54.398
having that on the way to work.

00:12:54.398 --> 00:12:57.193
I would walk into the office,
like, flipping tables.

00:12:57.193 --> 00:12:58.444
"Let's go!"

00:12:58.444 --> 00:13:01.781
Like, that sugar high would
just go right to my brain.

00:13:01.781 --> 00:13:04.992
And then 20 minutes
later, asleep at my desk.

00:13:04.992 --> 00:13:07.036
Americans love dessert for breakfast.

00:13:07.036 --> 00:13:07.870
We do, don't we?

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:10.039
You guys love pancakes, waffles,
doughnuts for breakfast.

00:13:10.039 --> 00:13:11.373
Joe: Our cereal is really sweet, right?

00:13:11.373 --> 00:13:13.334
So I don't know if you
can really draw the line

00:13:13.334 --> 00:13:15.002
at a rainbow bagel with cream cheese.

00:13:15.002 --> 00:13:19.215
But I will say, texture on
this bagel, really good.

00:13:19.215 --> 00:13:21.217
I would say this one
has maybe even more —

00:13:21.217 --> 00:13:22.259
Uh-oh.

00:13:22.259 --> 00:13:25.721
This one might have even more
of a kind of crispy outside.

00:13:25.721 --> 00:13:26.555
Joe: Than what?

00:13:26.555 --> 00:13:27.556
Than the previous one we just tried.

00:13:27.556 --> 00:13:29.225
Joe: So if you were to order again,

00:13:29.225 --> 00:13:31.018
you'd get this with the rainbow bagel?

00:13:31.018 --> 00:13:33.145
Harry: No, I think maybe
I just hope that they

00:13:33.145 --> 00:13:34.563
had a fresh poppy seed.

00:13:35.731 --> 00:13:38.317
Let me cleanse my palate
with a real bagel.

00:13:39.819 --> 00:13:42.446
Harry: Get some savory back
in you. Couple of capers.

00:13:42.446 --> 00:13:44.323
Back online. No time to waste, my man.

00:13:44.323 --> 00:13:45.324
Ready for the next bagel?

00:13:45.324 --> 00:13:46.659
I am, yeah. Can we run there

00:13:46.659 --> 00:13:48.369
so that I can burn off this energy high?

00:13:48.369 --> 00:13:50.287
Joe: That was the plan.
Harry: Cool. Sweet. Let's go.

00:13:55.334 --> 00:13:58.629
No, immediately not working.

00:13:58.629 --> 00:14:00.673
Joe: From Queens, we made
our way into Manhattan

00:14:00.673 --> 00:14:02.591
to continue our bagel tour.

00:14:02.591 --> 00:14:05.302
Next up, Tompkins Square Bagels.

00:14:05.302 --> 00:14:06.846
All right, we're at Tompkins Square Bagels

00:14:06.846 --> 00:14:09.306
right by the Tompkins
Square, in the East Village.

00:14:09.306 --> 00:14:10.641
This place is amazing.

00:14:10.641 --> 00:14:14.228
Also another spot that does
the kettle boil for the bagels.

00:14:14.228 --> 00:14:16.814
I haven't explained that
to you yet. I will now.

00:14:16.814 --> 00:14:18.941
Take the dough, throw
it in some boiling water

00:14:18.941 --> 00:14:19.775
right before they bake it.

00:14:19.775 --> 00:14:22.361
It gives the outside
of it that really nice,

00:14:22.361 --> 00:14:24.655
crisp, almost crackery crust.

00:14:24.655 --> 00:14:26.031
Nice. All I know about this place is that

00:14:26.031 --> 00:14:28.367
it's Timothée Chalamet's
favorite bagel in New York.

00:14:28.367 --> 00:14:29.368
And who is that?

00:14:29.368 --> 00:14:31.120
He's a guy.
Joe: OK.

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:32.580
Harry: Hit me with some bagel facts, Joe.

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:34.206
Joe: All right, bagel
facts. You ready? All right.

00:14:34.206 --> 00:14:35.833
Created by Eastern European
Jewish communities.

00:14:35.833 --> 00:14:37.668
Originated in Poland.

00:14:37.668 --> 00:14:38.502
Harry: Mm-hmm.

00:14:38.502 --> 00:14:40.838
It was food often given
to women in childbirth.

00:14:40.838 --> 00:14:44.049
The circle shape represents
life cycle and good luck.

00:14:44.049 --> 00:14:44.884
There you go.

00:14:44.884 --> 00:14:46.927
Truly is everything in a bagel, you know?

00:14:49.179 --> 00:14:50.848
Yeah.

00:14:50.848 --> 00:14:53.350
They made a reference to
the whole circle of life

00:14:53.350 --> 00:14:55.561
or death in "Everything
Everywhere All at Once."

00:14:55.561 --> 00:14:57.354
Yes. And "The Lion King."

00:14:57.354 --> 00:14:58.689
There's bagels in "The Lion King"?

00:14:58.689 --> 00:15:00.065
Harry: No, but there's a circle of life.

00:15:00.065 --> 00:15:01.525
Joe: Oh.
Harry: Yeah. Close enough.

00:15:01.525 --> 00:15:02.651
Joe: But there's a everything bagel in

00:15:02.651 --> 00:15:03.569
"Everything Everywhere All at Once."

00:15:03.569 --> 00:15:05.404
Harry: There is, correct.
Joe: Everything —

00:15:05.404 --> 00:15:07.406
I just made the connection
it's the everything.

00:15:07.406 --> 00:15:08.782
Harry: Mm-hmm.

00:15:08.782 --> 00:15:10.826
I can't help it, I can't
watch anything without, like,

00:15:10.826 --> 00:15:12.745
having two other screens in front of me.

00:15:12.745 --> 00:15:14.288
You need, like, "Family Guy" clips playing

00:15:14.288 --> 00:15:15.122
while you watch a movie.

00:15:15.122 --> 00:15:16.874
And at the end, I'm like,
"Yeah, yeah, I got it.

00:15:16.874 --> 00:15:17.708
Totally got it."

00:15:17.708 --> 00:15:18.709
Harry: "I got the gist. It's fine."

00:15:18.709 --> 00:15:20.169
Man, it's been, what, like, an hour

00:15:20.169 --> 00:15:21.670
since we last ate a bagel?

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:22.755
Joe: I don't know about
you, but I'm starving.

00:15:22.755 --> 00:15:24.548
Harry: I could go for a bagel right now.

00:15:30.512 --> 00:15:31.931
Joe: Oh-ho-ho-ho-ho-ho.

00:15:31.931 --> 00:15:34.642
Bro, what the hell! Are you kidding me?

00:15:34.642 --> 00:15:36.936
Harry: That thing is stacked!

00:15:36.936 --> 00:15:38.437
Thank you! Here we go.

00:15:38.437 --> 00:15:39.939
Joe: All right. So, I don't
know if you've noticed,

00:15:39.939 --> 00:15:42.149
but this one has got a lot of stuff.

00:15:42.149 --> 00:15:43.567
So this is a packed-out bagel.

00:15:43.567 --> 00:15:46.320
Joe: Cream cheese, sesame bagel.

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:47.446
Harry: Mm-hmm. Capers.

00:15:47.446 --> 00:15:49.698
Capers involved again.
Joe: Got them capey boys.

00:15:49.698 --> 00:15:51.325
Harry: It's got all the key elements.

00:15:51.325 --> 00:15:54.161
It's got the sesame seeds
on the top and the bottom.

00:15:54.161 --> 00:15:55.788
Joe: Yep. You've mentioned that before,

00:15:55.788 --> 00:15:56.789
where you're like, "Oh,
this stuff's on the top

00:15:56.789 --> 00:15:57.873
and the bottom." Like, it always is.

00:15:57.873 --> 00:15:59.249
Is it not like that in the UK?

00:15:59.249 --> 00:16:00.209
Not really, no.

00:16:00.209 --> 00:16:02.294
Joe: No! So you get a
bagel, it's just on the top?

00:16:02.294 --> 00:16:03.921
I mean, sesame bagels
aren't even that common.

00:16:03.921 --> 00:16:04.880
Some of them will be.

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:07.633
I feel like if you do get
one, often just on one side.

00:16:07.633 --> 00:16:08.467
That's not normal.

00:16:08.467 --> 00:16:11.345
Very hard to come by an everything
bagel in the UK as well.

00:16:11.345 --> 00:16:12.763
So in terms of the actual bagel options,

00:16:12.763 --> 00:16:14.640
we're pretty limited, whereas over here

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:15.766
you guys have got it figured out.

00:16:15.766 --> 00:16:17.434
This is beautiful. I just
kind of want to stare at this

00:16:17.434 --> 00:16:18.519
for a bit longer before I bite into it.

00:16:18.519 --> 00:16:19.937
I will say, I've said it
before and I'll say it again,

00:16:19.937 --> 00:16:21.563
I'm not big on the stacking.

00:16:21.563 --> 00:16:23.190
It's an aggressively stacked bagel.

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:25.317
Which makes it harder to
get everything on one bite.

00:16:25.317 --> 00:16:26.235
Harry: We'll see how it holds up.

00:16:26.235 --> 00:16:27.861
How big do they think my maw is?

00:16:27.861 --> 00:16:29.697
I mean, I've got a pretty
big mouth, so I'll be fine.

00:16:29.697 --> 00:16:30.823
Joe: Yeah. Not me, though.

00:16:30.823 --> 00:16:32.116
Harry: Good luck.
Joe: All right.

00:16:39.039 --> 00:16:39.999
OK.

00:16:41.041 --> 00:16:42.209
Mm.

00:16:43.544 --> 00:16:45.254
Joe: Cream cheese is definitely hitting.

00:16:45.254 --> 00:16:46.088
Mm-hmm.

00:16:46.088 --> 00:16:47.756
Joe: The salmon's fantastic.

00:16:48.966 --> 00:16:50.217
Could do a little less tomato.

00:16:50.217 --> 00:16:51.844
Not a fan of the tomato
or just too much of it?

00:16:51.844 --> 00:16:54.054
Joe: Not a fan of it. Like,
one slice would've been fine.

00:16:54.054 --> 00:16:55.347
Sure.

00:16:55.347 --> 00:16:57.641
Now, first time we've done sesame bagel.

00:16:58.726 --> 00:17:00.019
I'm liking it.

00:17:00.019 --> 00:17:02.229
The bagel itself, excellent.

00:17:03.063 --> 00:17:05.357
So, like, crispy-crunchy on the outside

00:17:06.233 --> 00:17:09.319
and a good amount of
sponginess on the inside.

00:17:09.319 --> 00:17:10.738
Joe: Oh, my God. And red onion?

00:17:10.738 --> 00:17:12.948
This is, like, the
onioniest one we've had.

00:17:12.948 --> 00:17:14.074
Harry: This is a very oniony bagel.

00:17:14.074 --> 00:17:15.909
Joe: And I am not complaining, buddy.

00:17:15.909 --> 00:17:17.286
I want to take just a
little bit of the salmon

00:17:17.286 --> 00:17:18.787
and just try that on its own as well.

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:22.583
Mm.

00:17:22.583 --> 00:17:25.669
So, I get more of a
smoked-salmon flavor from that,

00:17:25.669 --> 00:17:26.879
which personally I love.

00:17:26.879 --> 00:17:28.505
That's what I'm used to in the UK.

00:17:28.505 --> 00:17:29.423
If you get salmon like this,

00:17:29.423 --> 00:17:31.175
it's generally going to be smoked.

00:17:31.175 --> 00:17:33.218
I just think it adds such a lovely flavor.

00:17:33.218 --> 00:17:35.095
My thing's going sideways over here, bud.

00:17:36.055 --> 00:17:37.848
Ooh, just bit into a caper. Caper bomb.

00:17:37.848 --> 00:17:39.183
Harry: Caper bomb.

00:17:39.183 --> 00:17:40.934
I'm going in. I'm going back in.

00:17:43.812 --> 00:17:46.398
Joe: When I get everything
in a bite, fantastic.

00:17:46.398 --> 00:17:47.941
Really impressed with the bagel.

00:17:47.941 --> 00:17:49.359
Of all the bagels we've had,

00:17:49.359 --> 00:17:51.236
maybe the crispiest on the outside.

00:17:51.236 --> 00:17:52.821
Harry: Yeah, the crackle
as you bite into that

00:17:52.821 --> 00:17:54.823
from the outside is so satisfying.

00:17:54.823 --> 00:17:56.575
Joe: It's onion overload,

00:17:56.575 --> 00:18:00.579
which might be overshadowing
the lox ever so slightly.

00:18:00.579 --> 00:18:02.956
I got so much s--- in my teeth right now.

00:18:02.956 --> 00:18:04.458
Harry: That's the
downside to sesame seeds.

00:18:04.458 --> 00:18:06.418
Zoom in. Show me the monitor.

00:18:06.418 --> 00:18:08.462
Harry: Oh, God. [laughs]

00:18:09.755 --> 00:18:10.756
I got it.

00:18:10.756 --> 00:18:12.508
Harry: One of my few
complaints about this is that

00:18:12.508 --> 00:18:14.760
maybe they've done a little bit too much

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:16.011
because it's a slightly messy eat.

00:18:16.011 --> 00:18:20.015
This was the original Xbox thickness

00:18:20.015 --> 00:18:21.892
of bagel stacking that we've
had today. And I get it.

00:18:21.892 --> 00:18:24.394
Some people, they're like,
"Yeah, I want a lot."

00:18:24.394 --> 00:18:26.772
One thing I wanted you
to try they have here

00:18:26.772 --> 00:18:30.484
is, how can I put this? Pastrami lox?

00:18:30.484 --> 00:18:32.861
Those are two words that
don't really exist in the UK.

00:18:32.861 --> 00:18:34.029
Joe: Figure out a way to make it together.

00:18:34.029 --> 00:18:35.531
You want to check it out?
Harry: Absolutely.

00:18:35.531 --> 00:18:37.116
Joe: So it is, look at that,

00:18:37.116 --> 00:18:40.452
it's salmon that is
prepared like pastrami.

00:18:40.452 --> 00:18:43.539
They got the pepper crust. Right?

00:18:43.539 --> 00:18:44.414
Get a load of that.

00:18:44.414 --> 00:18:46.375
So, as someone who's kind of, like,

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:50.754
as someone who's pretty new to pastrami,

00:18:52.256 --> 00:18:54.967
what types of flavors
can I be expecting here?

00:18:54.967 --> 00:18:56.009
This is a pastrami rub,

00:18:56.009 --> 00:18:58.637
so it's going to be very pepper heavy,

00:18:58.637 --> 00:19:00.264
but kind of a smokiness to it.

00:19:00.264 --> 00:19:01.765
I think that's where you're
getting the smokiness from.

00:19:01.765 --> 00:19:04.184
Because this was, this does
smell like it's been smoked.

00:19:04.184 --> 00:19:05.144
Harry: Sure.

00:19:05.144 --> 00:19:07.271
But that's not the exact salmon

00:19:07.271 --> 00:19:08.480
that we've had on the bagel, right?

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:10.440
No, sir, this is different.

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:11.608
Ooh, this is so good.

00:19:14.528 --> 00:19:15.779
Mm.

00:19:15.779 --> 00:19:16.738
Joe: This is so good.

00:19:16.738 --> 00:19:18.782
Does have a pastrami flavor to it, right?

00:19:18.782 --> 00:19:19.908
Harry: It does.

00:19:19.908 --> 00:19:21.076
Weird how they did that.

00:19:21.076 --> 00:19:23.745
Harry: This is going to be
a slight insult to pastrami,

00:19:23.745 --> 00:19:25.372
but the thing it reminds
me of is almost, like,

00:19:25.372 --> 00:19:26.665
a hot-dog flavor.

00:19:26.665 --> 00:19:29.001
Like, the frankfurter kind of seasoning.

00:19:29.001 --> 00:19:30.127
I get that.

00:19:30.127 --> 00:19:31.879
I will say this is nicer than a hot dog.

00:19:31.879 --> 00:19:32.963
It's fantastic. I mean,
I want to have more,

00:19:32.963 --> 00:19:34.423
but I think you guys
should try some of this.

00:19:34.423 --> 00:19:36.008
Let's do Conner first and then, yeah,

00:19:36.008 --> 00:19:38.135
let's get him, rip a bong hit on there.

00:19:39.928 --> 00:19:41.597
Right? That tastes exactly like pastrami.

00:19:41.597 --> 00:19:42.723
You're next.

00:19:46.143 --> 00:19:47.227
It does kind of taste like a hot dog.

00:19:47.227 --> 00:19:48.437
Joe: It tastes like pastrami.

00:19:48.437 --> 00:19:49.688
Harry: Thank you. Hot dog.

00:19:49.688 --> 00:19:50.814
Yeah, thank you.

00:19:50.814 --> 00:19:53.400
I would be really tempted
to try that on my bagel

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:54.818
instead of just the standard lox.

00:19:54.818 --> 00:19:57.404
Joe: Go. Go. Go. Put it on there.

00:19:57.404 --> 00:19:58.405
Remake it.

00:20:03.368 --> 00:20:04.286
Yeah?

00:20:04.286 --> 00:20:05.120
Mm-hmm.

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:06.455
Don't get too full on bagelness.

00:20:06.455 --> 00:20:08.415
We got another spot to go to.
And I don't know about you,

00:20:08.415 --> 00:20:11.335
but I'm [burps] hungry for bagels, baby.

00:20:11.335 --> 00:20:14.046
I could eat an almost
infinite number of bagels.

00:20:14.046 --> 00:20:14.880
So let's go.

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:15.714
Joe: Let's go.

00:20:23.263 --> 00:20:26.016
Our fourth and final stop was
to a New York bagel staple

00:20:26.016 --> 00:20:28.060
since 1976.

00:20:28.060 --> 00:20:29.436
Ess-a-Bagel.

00:20:29.436 --> 00:20:33.774
This might be the most
localest favoritest spot.

00:20:33.774 --> 00:20:36.193
And the most historic. It's
been here since the '70s.

00:20:36.193 --> 00:20:37.277
Started by an Austrian family.

00:20:37.277 --> 00:20:39.112
They have a bunch of locations now.

00:20:39.112 --> 00:20:40.322
This isn't the first one.

00:20:40.322 --> 00:20:41.740
Harry: It's a nice spot.
Joe: Yeah, it's a nice spot.

00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:42.574
Lot of real estate here.

00:20:42.574 --> 00:20:44.201
Joe: Yeah. Right by
Penn Station. Very nice.

00:20:44.201 --> 00:20:45.577
Convenient to get to.

00:20:45.577 --> 00:20:47.579
I want to see what the bagels are like.

00:20:47.579 --> 00:20:49.248
Last one of the day.

00:20:49.248 --> 00:20:50.123
Here we go.

00:20:50.123 --> 00:20:53.543
Ooh, baby. Oh, mama.

00:20:53.543 --> 00:20:55.587
Joe: Want to call to your attention

00:20:55.587 --> 00:20:56.964
to the first and only appearance

00:20:56.964 --> 00:21:00.801
of lettuce in our bagels.
Harry: Lettuce. In a bagel.

00:21:00.801 --> 00:21:02.594
Is this common? Obviously, I guess not,

00:21:02.594 --> 00:21:04.179
because it hasn't been in
any of the other places.

00:21:04.179 --> 00:21:05.013
Joe: No, it is not.

00:21:05.013 --> 00:21:06.515
Harry: How do you feel
about lettuce in a bagel?

00:21:06.515 --> 00:21:08.892
Joe: I can't imagine the
lettuce swaying my opinion

00:21:08.892 --> 00:21:10.185
too much on it, I don't think.

00:21:10.185 --> 00:21:13.063
But I'll reserve that official POV

00:21:13.063 --> 00:21:14.523
until after I bite into this.

00:21:14.523 --> 00:21:15.607
Harry: Yeah. I think, like,

00:21:15.607 --> 00:21:17.442
maybe this has one of the higher

00:21:17.442 --> 00:21:20.070
filling-to-bread ratios
that we've had so far.

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:20.904
Joe: Oh, yeah. Good point.

00:21:20.904 --> 00:21:22.906
Harry: This thing is
almost entirely filling.

00:21:22.906 --> 00:21:24.157
Not a ton of bagel.

00:21:24.157 --> 00:21:26.076
Joe: Not complaining.
Here we go. Bite. Go.

00:21:26.076 --> 00:21:27.411
Harry: All right, man.

00:21:33.333 --> 00:21:35.168
Mm.

00:21:35.168 --> 00:21:37.838
I was wrong. I can really
taste the lettuce on this.

00:21:38.839 --> 00:21:40.549
You do get the lettuce, yeah.

00:21:40.549 --> 00:21:42.384
If I may.

00:21:42.384 --> 00:21:44.553
Harry: Ooh, the lettuce is coming out.

00:21:44.553 --> 00:21:46.305
The lettuce has been removed.

00:21:49.850 --> 00:21:52.853
I will say we've had
a lot of bagels today,

00:21:52.853 --> 00:21:55.188
and I feel like both of us
are still devouring these.

00:21:55.188 --> 00:21:56.773
So make of that what you will.

00:21:58.150 --> 00:22:01.320
Points off for the lettuce,
but everything else,

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:03.196
really generous with the
lox, don't you agree?

00:22:03.196 --> 00:22:05.741
Harry: Yeah, it's thickly cut.
Joe: Thickly cut, right?

00:22:05.741 --> 00:22:07.075
I thought so, too.

00:22:07.075 --> 00:22:10.746
That's thickly cut. The
salmon tastes fresh.

00:22:10.746 --> 00:22:12.748
Not, like, in-your-face fishy, but, like,

00:22:12.748 --> 00:22:14.333
you can taste it, which is good.

00:22:14.333 --> 00:22:15.459
Joe: I really love the thick lox.

00:22:15.459 --> 00:22:16.710
I'm really impressed with it.

00:22:19.087 --> 00:22:21.590
Mm! Mm!

00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:22.841
I'd put this cream cheese again at, like,

00:22:22.841 --> 00:22:24.801
the kind of gentler end of the scale,

00:22:24.801 --> 00:22:26.595
which I know you've kind
of suggested that you like.

00:22:26.595 --> 00:22:28.597
You know, I want the, like, too much of a

00:22:28.597 --> 00:22:30.307
sour cream cheese flavor.
Joe: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:22:30.307 --> 00:22:31.475
Harry: The cook on the bagel is great.

00:22:31.475 --> 00:22:34.061
You see these little
kind of blistery bits?

00:22:34.061 --> 00:22:35.270
You can tell it's been kettled.

00:22:35.270 --> 00:22:37.731
Gets that real nice crust on the outside.

00:22:37.731 --> 00:22:38.982
If I was to get this again,

00:22:38.982 --> 00:22:41.276
don't put lettuce anywhere near my bagel.

00:22:41.276 --> 00:22:42.903
It's enough of biting something

00:22:42.903 --> 00:22:43.862
and everything going this way.

00:22:43.862 --> 00:22:46.740
I think lettuce is, like,
the, what do you call that?

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:49.451
Emulsifier of that. It's just like,

00:22:49.451 --> 00:22:51.912
lettuce is like, "All right,
boys, we're out of here."

00:22:51.912 --> 00:22:54.206
It's like when you try and, like,

00:22:54.206 --> 00:22:56.708
pull the tablecloth out from the table

00:22:56.708 --> 00:22:58.251
and everything just comes off with it.

00:22:58.251 --> 00:23:00.337
I feel like that's the effect
that the lettuce is having.

00:23:00.337 --> 00:23:02.672
That's a much better analogy.

00:23:02.672 --> 00:23:04.633
Overall, really good. Really good.

00:23:04.633 --> 00:23:06.426
Overall, still a very good bagel.

00:23:06.426 --> 00:23:09.596
Now is the part of the episode

00:23:09.596 --> 00:23:12.974
where we do the thing that
we always do at the end.

00:23:12.974 --> 00:23:13.809
It's true.

00:23:13.809 --> 00:23:16.144
We're going to decide
who had the best bagel.

00:23:16.144 --> 00:23:18.313
All right. For me, I was looking for

00:23:18.313 --> 00:23:20.273
a combination of everything
in a bite. Right?

00:23:20.273 --> 00:23:21.691
So I was not very happy with stacking

00:23:21.691 --> 00:23:22.859
at some of these places.

00:23:22.859 --> 00:23:24.069
Obviously these are bagel places,

00:23:24.069 --> 00:23:26.154
so every bagel had to be nice and pillowy,

00:23:26.154 --> 00:23:27.781
but also crispy on the outside.

00:23:27.781 --> 00:23:29.908
I think almost every bagel
we had today was fresh.

00:23:29.908 --> 00:23:30.826
Harry: Mm-hmm.
Joe: Right?

00:23:30.826 --> 00:23:33.245
I don't recall disliking any of the lox

00:23:33.245 --> 00:23:34.746
at any of the places
we've been to, do you?

00:23:34.746 --> 00:23:36.289
No. I think we've had varying levels

00:23:36.289 --> 00:23:40.377
of smokiness versus just
straight lox flavor.

00:23:40.377 --> 00:23:41.920
But overall, I think I agree.

00:23:41.920 --> 00:23:45.674
Yeah, we've had fresh bagels,
high-quality ingredients

00:23:45.674 --> 00:23:47.300
pretty much across the board.

00:23:47.300 --> 00:23:50.095
It's a difficult decision.
I would say, broadly,

00:23:50.095 --> 00:23:53.890
I've had some of the best
bagels of my life today.

00:23:53.890 --> 00:23:55.016
When have you had a better bagel

00:23:55.016 --> 00:23:56.101
than the bagels you had today?

00:23:56.101 --> 00:23:57.227
What city were you in?

00:23:57.227 --> 00:23:58.395
Harry: I don't think I have.
Joe: OK.

00:23:58.395 --> 00:23:59.438
I do think that on average,

00:23:59.438 --> 00:24:01.982
these definitely outrank
UK bagels, unfortunately.

00:24:01.982 --> 00:24:03.108
Joe: Yeah, I'd say so too.

00:24:03.108 --> 00:24:05.026
Harry: Whether it's the tap water,

00:24:05.026 --> 00:24:06.945
the famous New York tap
water or something else.

00:24:06.945 --> 00:24:10.031
But I wish that we
could take these bagels,

00:24:10.031 --> 00:24:10.991
make them in the UK,

00:24:10.991 --> 00:24:13.160
because I would be eating these so often.

00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:13.994
Joe: It's amazing, right?

00:24:13.994 --> 00:24:14.953
Yeah. Really, really good.

00:24:14.953 --> 00:24:16.538
I'll just go and say right now, then,

00:24:16.538 --> 00:24:19.166
my favorite bagel today was,

00:24:19.166 --> 00:24:21.585
and which bagel of the two,
I'm just going to pick ...

00:24:21.585 --> 00:24:23.086
Tompkins Square.

00:24:23.086 --> 00:24:23.920
Harry: Nice.
Joe: Yeah.

00:24:23.920 --> 00:24:26.131
There was just something
about the bagel and the lox.

00:24:26.131 --> 00:24:27.466
Everything worked so well together.

00:24:27.466 --> 00:24:29.593
I mean, we're really talking
about, out of all four of them,

00:24:29.593 --> 00:24:33.013
we're talking millimeters
better as far as, like,

00:24:33.013 --> 00:24:36.558
however we're ranking them,
we're measuring them, but,

00:24:36.558 --> 00:24:39.144
so but Tompkins was just, like,
now that we've eaten all day

00:24:39.144 --> 00:24:40.812
and I've had a chance to think about it,

00:24:40.812 --> 00:24:41.855
fantastic bagels there.

00:24:41.855 --> 00:24:43.106
Chalamet knows his stuff, huh?

00:24:43.106 --> 00:24:44.441
Joe: Yeah, that's right.

00:24:44.441 --> 00:24:45.942
Chally boy.

00:24:45.942 --> 00:24:47.235
Again, it's like,

00:24:47.235 --> 00:24:48.403
there's a hair in this.

00:24:48.403 --> 00:24:49.404
Joe: Yeah.

00:24:50.405 --> 00:24:51.531
I think it's Utopia for me.

00:24:51.531 --> 00:24:53.575
Joe: Wow. OK. All right.

00:24:53.575 --> 00:24:56.369
Harry: Those were really,
really good bagels.

00:24:56.369 --> 00:24:58.205
It was the fact that they had
just come out of the oven.

00:24:58.205 --> 00:25:00.373
They're churning out fresh bagels

00:25:00.373 --> 00:25:01.625
all throughout the day.

00:25:01.625 --> 00:25:03.251
You can go in there pretty much anytime,

00:25:03.251 --> 00:25:06.755
breakfast, lunch, dinner,
and get a hot, fresh bagel.

00:25:06.755 --> 00:25:08.465
I think the ratio of fillings was good,

00:25:08.465 --> 00:25:10.091
ingredients were good quality,

00:25:10.091 --> 00:25:12.594
everything in that bagel
just worked for me,

00:25:12.594 --> 00:25:13.929
and I think it was excellent.

00:25:13.929 --> 00:25:16.264
Considering that we've eaten
quite a lot of bagel today,

00:25:16.264 --> 00:25:17.557
I'm still feeling pretty good.

00:25:17.557 --> 00:25:21.186
Joe: I am stuffed to the
gills. I'm never eating again.

00:25:21.186 --> 00:25:22.938
[Joe laughs]

